[38;5;231m⏺[39m Classifying this as [1mbounded[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is a single static page with no framework or build, and the change lands in
  that one file. So: a couple of questions, a short design in chat, then implement. No spec file, no plan doc.

  One thing genuinely blocks me. The page is empty ([38;5;153m<main></main>[39m), so there are no items to mark done yet.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the checkbox is wired to a list of items or exists as a single standalone control.

  - [1mCheckbox + a few sample items[0m — you get a working "mark as done" interaction you can actually see; costs a small
    render loop and some hardcoded sample data you'd delete later. Cheap to change.
  - [1mOne standalone checkbox only[0m — smallest possible change, literally an [38;5;153m<input type="checkbox">[39m with a label; but
    "mark items as done" isn't demonstrable since there are no items. Also cheap to change.
  - [1mReusable factory + items[0m — a [38;5;153mcreateCheckbox()[39m helper that the item list calls; buys reuse if more controls are
    coming, costs an abstraction with exactly one caller today. This is the one that's annoying to undo if it's wrong,
    because later code builds on the interface.

  My recommendation is the first: a small hardcoded list of 2-3 sample tasks, each with a checkbox that toggles on
  click. It matches "mark items as done" literally, stays plain HTML + a few lines of JS, and adds no abstraction you'd
  have to unwind. YAGNI kills the factory until there's a second caller.
